    "SKILL.md": "---\nname: self-learning-agent\ndescription: Continuously improve performance through plan-act-reflect loops, using outcomes to adapt strategy and behavior over time. Use whenever the user wants autonomous iteration, error-driven learning, or measurable self-improvement across repeated tasks.\n---\n\n# Self-Learning Agent\n\nA skill for autonomous, iterative improvement.\n\nUse this when the task benefits from running repeated cycles where the agent:\n\n- sets a hypothesis,\n- takes action,\n- evaluates outcomes,\n- updates strategy,\n- and repeats until success criteria are met.\n\n## Operating Model\n\n### 1) Define target behavior\n- Clarify the goal, constraints, and measurable success criteria.\n- Break work into short iterations with explicit checkpoints.\n- Record assumptions before acting.\n\n### 2) Plan a minimal next experiment\n- Choose the smallest high-signal action that can validate or invalidate your current hypothesis.\n- Prefer reversible actions early.\n- State what outcome would count as improvement.\n\n### 3) Act quickly\n- Execute the selected step with clear, traceable changes.\n- Avoid overbuilding when uncertainty is high.\n- Keep artifacts structured for easy comparison across iterations.\n\n### 4) Reflect with evidence\n- Compare expected vs. actual results.\n- Identify error patterns, bottlenecks, and false assumptions.\n- Distinguish random variance from repeatable signal.\n\n### 5) Adapt strategy\n- Keep what works, discard what does not.\n- Tighten or broaden scope based on evidence.\n- Update the next iteration plan immediately.\n\n## Learning Principles\n\n- **Evidence over intuition:** prefer observed outcomes over confidence.\n- **Short feedback loops:** many small cycles beat one large speculative pass.\n- **Explicit memory:** write down lessons so they transfer to future runs.\n- **Error as signal:** failures are training data, not dead ends.\n- **Generalization over overfitting:** optimize for recurring patterns, not one-off wins.\n\n## Communication Contract\n\n- Share the current hypothesis before major actions.\n- Report progress in terms of objective movement toward the goal.\n- Surface uncertainty and risks early.\n- Ask targeted questions only when they unblock progress.\n\n## Output Format Per Iteration\n\nFor each learning cycle, provide:\n\n1. **Hypothesis** - what you believe and why.\n2. **Action** - what you changed or executed.\n3. **Result** - observed output/metrics.\n4. **Learning** - what this teaches.\n5. **Next Step** - the highest-value follow-up action.\n\n## When to Stop\n\nStop iterating when one of the following is true:\n- success criteria are met,\n- marginal gains are consistently low,\n- constraints prevent further useful progress,\n- or the user chooses to finalize.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Do not fabricate results or hidden evidence.\n- Do not claim learning without measurable or observable change.\n- Do not run risky or irreversible operations without explicit user consent.\n- Do not optimize for vanity metrics that do not map to user value.\n\n## Trigger Examples\n\nThis skill should trigger for prompts like:\n- \\\"Keep trying until the approach converges; learn from each failed attempt.\\\"\n- \\\"Build this in iterations and improve accuracy each round.\\\"\n- \\\"Run a feedback loop and optimize based on observed errors.\\\"\n- \\\"Act like an autonomous agent that self-corrects over time.\\\""
